cause i  actually wanted to make hollywood films  or that i thought i was even capable of  doing it but because  i studied religion as an undergraduate i  knew i was always interested in film i'd  been making little  films since i was in high school but i  needed to have formal training and i  needed to get my hands on a camera  and i needed to be able to work with  people who i wasn't going to pay  to deal with me and vice versa so  film school was my my gave me the  ability to do that and  um but then there was you know in my  pursuit of trying to understand  understand the tradition i think myself  along with many others  you get to a point in any program any  kind of graduate program i think where  you get really frustrated and  self-defeating because  you're not doing it right you know  you're like why am i not and especially  when you're younger you're so focused on  your worth is about how well you can  master something how well you  can essentially fit into the box yes how  how well can you draw into the lines you  know what i mean  and that becomes some kind of foray or  some kind of  indication of if you're worthy of it or  not you know  and that's when your confidence starts  to get bad and you start to hate the  program  and then you know things happen and this  happens to all of us i think i  understand this so deeply  yes yeah i feel really grateful because  in that moment  of feeling defeated in that moment of  not being able to understand  you know especially with my dyslexia  like crossing the line and like doing  all those like things it was  like i can't do this i'm not doing it  like it was just so hard you know  in that moment it really i tried to use  it as a as a prompt  to further understand myself to that it  was actually  it was a gift because it was something  from which i could bounce off of to  further see myself  and to further articulate what i can do  and what i want to do and what i'm  interested in doing  and i think it was actually similar to  you know when i was younger when i was  in high school  you know a lot of this is a very  gendered experience and i'm hoping that  um  i think it's changed even since since  you and i were younger but  uh you know then like a lot of the guys  were really good at coming together and  working together and helping each other  out  absolutely you know yeah and i feel like  myself and a lot of my other girlfriends  were more independent and so  i really had to learn how to if i was  going to be interested in making films  i needed to learn how to do them by  myself without a crew or a team  um and that forced me into  a certain methodology that was really  reliant on what i could do on my own  that was really reliant on this idea  of questions as questions being equally  as important as answers in some ways  and it's what helped me ultimately to as  you mentioned  find the confidence to be curious right  so those two things kind of conflated  is is how i developed  myself you know um and it wasn't it i  was i was never invested in trying to go  against the grain and trying to do  something that was different  i'm not actually interested in like  rebellion in that way  um it truly is just uh  who i am it's the only way i know how to  do it you know then you get to a certain  point where you  start to say okay i feel like i've  exercised this muscle what what other  muscles can i exercise and that's where  i think for me it gets really exciting  because that's where the collaborative  process  needs to open up that's where you start  to bring in more people and you work  with  brilliant human beings who who are  bringing so much and helping you to  elevate the work  in a way that you yourself you know  could never do by your  you know on your own that's what  filmmaking is it's collaborative you  know  that's really beautiful garrett and it  resonates so deeply  when i was in undergrad i found myself  trying to emulate  everyone i saw around me i understood  how you make a great photo within  photojournalism my undergraduate degree  was in photojournalism  and i i felt like i couldn't breathe i  felt like i was losing who i was  and i had to take a time of unlearning  everything removing myself from  all the education i had i took a break  within my  during my second year of grad school  because i wanted to fail  i wanted to mess up i wanted to return  to my own personal roots to really  allow myself to rebirth who i was  supposed to be  not thinking about anyone else what  success is supposed to look like  um what i've been taught but also i  really had been trained to do  everything you learn how to do the audio  you learn how to  do the cinematography the editing and i  do think that was something that was  really helpful in me  discovering my voice by understanding  the importance of each element  i understood what i liked um and so  now it's so fun for me to collaborate  because i'm like oh now i understand who  i  am and i want to accept and embrace who  you are and see how we can put these  these two worlds together um it's  it's so true though that men have always  looked out for one another within school  within the industry  and it's really been inspiring to me and  exciting for me to see  you know women taking over women women  taking their their rightful place within  the world of cinema it's long overdue  and  it really is i remember watching your  film garrett  wanting to see what it did because i was  rooting for it i was like this needs to  get to this place we need to make sure  we have  black women storytellers at this level  not saying this level is the reason  we're good not saying this  acknowledgement  um makes us better but understanding  what it means in the sense of how we  get to continue doing the work  understanding how it provides resources  understanding how it allows us to  continue  telling these stories yes yes so well  said you know speaking of women i think  both of us have an affinity  um for uh women's voices for women's  stories  and i'm curious because i'm always asked  about this and i'm curious for you  is that intentional or is that intuitive  especially when we talk about love song  philatasha like what  what brought you to that story and  was it connected to something that is  that is broader  um that is even bigger that is connected  to sort of overall intention  with your work it's intuitive and i  think the way you answered about  you know this is just who i am when when  you create your work  i don't know how to do anything else  this is what i know  this is what i love these are the  stories i care about  and and i'm not ashamed to be dedicated  to that i don't  want to ever act like i'm someone that  wants to tell  all of these stories like no i love  telling stories about women i love  telling stories about  black girls black women you know queer  folks  and for me a love song for latasha  it felt very close to home because i am  a native of los angeles i was a young  girl during the riots  and i always associated it with rodney  king so for me  to witness this erasure happen in  history where latasha's name  always existed within that trauma i  really wanted to  to disrupt that archive and to provide  healing for black women  healing for the community and  i wanted us to have a tangible record i  wanted it this to be a spiritual archive  for people to know how important her  existence was  how important her memory is and what it  looks like for us to continue moving  forward in  our um dismantling of anti-blackness  and our dismantling of the erasure of  black woman  and in acknowledging the adultification  that happens to young black girls  but i didn't approach this film thinking  i  was being political i don't think that's  ever my thought i'm never trying to be  political  but i think i can't remove the political  aspect from the story  for me it was always this is a story  that needs to exist i  want to be a part of this journey with  tai and shanice  to rebirth these memories to give them a  place to live  tangibly and as i mentioned when my dad  passed when i was a young girl i was the  same age as latasha so i've always had  a desire to tell a story about a 15 year  old black girl and i just didn't  understand this is the moment  that it would come together this is the  moment that it would happen and  something i do want to mention garrett  that i think is so interesting  about both of our films this is  understanding of time  you know thinking about your title but  also  the whole concept of serving time  of moving through time of time being  non-linear  we both are dealing with stories that  were deeply deeply ingrained in the past  but we're watching this rebirth we're  watching a moment happen where that  archive gets to finally live  and it makes me think about um a quote  that my mentor opiemio leukemia shared  with me the future informs the past  and so watching your film i was like i  know  i i doubt they ever thought when we were  creating this archive that one day we  would meet garrett bradley  and she would present this to the world  and it would make sense why  sybil was creating these these videos  why this record was so important  and so i'd love you for you to just  speak about your own personal  exploration of time  what it meant to experience time with  the family  and just how you all navigated your own  understandings of time  first and foremost like i came to  to meet fox and to meet robert and to  meet the whole family on the premise of  um the absence of understanding and  images  around the effects of incarceration  right the  effects of and speaking of ava when we  think about her film 13th right which is  um a critical content you know  um that helps people understand the  connection between slavery in our  country  and the prison industrial complex right  and i feel like time stands on the  shoulders of a film like 13th and it  show it's the other half of the circle  in the sense that it is speaking to now  understanding that on the on the the  micro level  understanding it on the human the human  level  the emotional level you know first and  foremost  that is kind of like where i came in and  it was a lot of conversations with them  in the beginning with the family around  why do we want to make this film you  know  and and and fox and rob were very clear  with me and saying you know our story is  the story of 2.3 million american  families  and we feel that our story can offer  hope and so i felt like  you know my job was to try to distill  and understand what hope  meant for them and what it looked like  in their daily life in ritual and  routine  um how was that articulated in  actionable terms in cinematic terms  and that inevitably encompassed the  concept of time  right the concept of of all tenses of  time  the the construct of time time as a  weapon right  time is something that is also um both  very concrete and abstract  all at once and and the thing that that  ultimately i think i've taken away from  this and you know to kind of answer your  question like  those hope ended up looking like three  things it ended up looking like love  you know it ended up looking like unity  the ability for them to stay together  over the course of  two decades without being physically  connected to one another which is  something that families  who are physically under the same  household sometimes don't have  you know um and it was being an  individual none of them is  are are defined by the system in any  kind of way and  the beauty of that is love and unity  and and individualism are not bound by  time  they're not bound by linearity or or  chronology they surpass  all of those things and so that allowed  us you know particularly with the  archive which my not knowing that the  archive existed until our last day of  filming  and having to completely rethink not the  intention  but the structure of the film yes i  still had anchoring points that were  rooted  into something that was transparent and  agreed upon collectively with the family  you know which was hope which was the  the three articulations of hope for them  and so that's a really big answer but at  the end of the day it's a love  it's it's about two people and and their  children and  and how they manage to get through two  decades of hell  in these very mundane and almost  quotidian ways that  anybody can use anyone can can  can uh can fight with and that people  actively are  you know 2.3 million americans actively  are if not double triple quadruple that  number if you take into account the  family  it is truly is a love story yes it's a  love story and i think your film  also is is truly it's truly a testament  of love to latasha to black girls to  to the potential of what america can be  and should be uh  to the form of cinema so i just want to  say thank you  so much for like speaking with me today  and i and i  will be together in person and we'll be  celebrating  not only each other and our films but  everyone who helped make them come to  fruition and  uh thank you for your work and i just  can't wait to to see everything you're  going to be doing and no pressure no  rush but i'm ready  thank you for saying no rush that's the  sweetest thing someone could say garrett  this is such a joy such an honor  i am truly a deep fan of your work i  love  watching you create i am going to  continue being a fan of your work and  a friend to walk through this industry  with you  but thank you so much for spending this  time with me 30 minutes is not enough  time  you
 

